1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:01,750
Your hands are mirror images.

2
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:04,450
This year's Nobel
Prize in Chemistry

3
00:00:04,613 --> 00:00:06,457
is about how one
hand can take over.

4
00:00:07,327 --> 00:00:08,447
Many molecules come in

5
00:00:08,447 --> 00:00:09,487
two hands like that.

6
00:00:09,907 --> 00:00:12,133
Life is picky: your proteins use

7
00:00:12,179 --> 00:00:14,264
only one hand of
each amino acid.

8
00:00:15,124 --> 00:00:16,643
Yet a reaction left to itself

9
00:00:16,704 --> 00:00:18,834
makes both hands, fifty-fifty.

10
00:00:19,344 --> 00:00:21,424
A one-handed catalyst,
a helper that

11
00:00:21,448 --> 00:00:23,417
isn't used up, can
tip the balance.

12
00:00:24,317 --> 00:00:25,575
In nineteen fifty-three,

13
00:00:25,677 --> 00:00:27,297
Charles Frank wrote a recipe for

14
00:00:27,351 --> 00:00:29,021
one hand to take
over completely.

15
00:00:29,477 --> 00:00:32,509
One: a one-handed
catalyst; chemists had

16
00:00:32,554 --> 00:00:34,374
that by the early
nineteen hundreds.

17
00:00:34,777 --> 00:00:36,410
Two: boost one hand and

18
00:00:36,457 --> 00:00:37,477
hold back the other.

19
00:00:37,897 --> 00:00:39,442
Three: a reaction that

20
00:00:39,475 --> 00:00:40,685
makes its own catalyst.

21
00:00:41,945 --> 00:00:43,165
In nineteen eighty-six,

22
00:00:43,355 --> 00:00:45,165
Henri Kagan ticked box two.

23
00:00:45,625 --> 00:00:45,797
His

24
00:00:45,846 --> 00:00:46,315
insight:

25
00:00:46,805 --> 00:00:53,305
the catalyst's metal holds two pieces, so mixed hands make
three kinds of pairs, and the mixed pair barely works.

26
00:00:53,815 --> 00:00:56,172
Start seventy-five to
twenty-five, and the

27
00:00:56,220 --> 00:00:56,531
working

28
00:00:56,579 --> 00:01:00,942
catalysts are ninety to ten: a purer
product than a straight line predicts.

29
00:01:02,591 --> 00:01:03,811
In nineteen ninety-five,

30
00:01:04,021 --> 00:01:06,001
Kenso Soai ticked box three:

31
00:01:06,461 --> 00:01:06,538
a

32
00:01:06,587 --> 00:01:11,117
molecule that helps build more of itself,
same hand, from simple ingredients.

33
00:01:12,027 --> 00:01:14,229
In two thousand three,
his team started

34
00:01:14,258 --> 00:01:16,537
with a lead of five
parts in ten million.

35
00:01:17,087 --> 00:01:19,167
Each run's product
seeds the next:

36
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:21,447
fifty-seven percent
after one run,

37
00:01:21,637 --> 00:01:23,067
ninety-nine after two,

38
00:01:23,271 --> 00:01:25,420
past ninety-nine and
a half after three.

39
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:29,520
Then, with no one-handed
ingredient added,

40
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:31,370
the reaction still
picked a hand,

41
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:32,210
at random.

42
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:34,668
In thirty-seven runs, nineteen

43
00:01:34,716 --> 00:01:36,621
leaned one way,
eighteen the other.

44
00:01:37,511 --> 00:01:39,657
In our toy model,
copying alone isn't

45
00:01:39,686 --> 00:01:42,361
enough: ten thousand
runs end up anywhere.

46
00:01:42,874 --> 00:01:45,019
Add box two, where
a one-hand and

47
00:01:45,043 --> 00:01:46,820
a mirror molecule
pair up and stop

48
00:01:46,861 --> 00:01:48,669
working, and nearly every run

49
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:50,471
ends with one hand taking over.

50
00:01:50,847 --> 00:01:52,440
Which hand is a coin toss.

51
00:01:54,710 --> 00:01:56,960
A toy, not how life
chose its hand.

52
00:01:57,440 --> 00:01:59,770
The Nobel Committee
stresses Frank's model

53
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:02,488
isn't the answer to
life's origin, and how

54
00:02:02,587 --> 00:02:04,698
Soai's reaction works
is still debated.

55
00:02:05,598 --> 00:02:07,618
Choosing the hand
matters for medicines:

56
00:02:08,028 --> 00:02:09,398
one form does the job,

57
00:02:09,698 --> 00:02:09,748
and

58
00:02:09,772 --> 00:02:14,447
its mirror image can cause unnecessary,
sometimes harmful, side effects.

59
00:02:15,687 --> 00:02:17,025
Race the mirrors yourself

60
00:02:17,073 --> 00:02:19,917
in our open Nobel 2026 lab.

61
00:02:20,507 --> 00:02:23,063
Explained with
showtime, an open-source

62
00:02:23,147 --> 00:02:24,961
video studio for coding agents.
